1
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,100
I know what you're thinking.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,900
You're wondering how I got here,
standing at the edge of everything.

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,600
The truth is, nobody plans
for something like this.

4
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:19,300
It started three months ago,
in a city that never sleeps.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,800
I was just a programmer,
writing code for a living.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,500
Nothing special. Nothing heroic.
Just another face in the crowd.

7
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:30,400
Then the signal appeared.
A pattern hidden in the noise.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,200
At first I thought it was a glitch.
A bug in the system.

9
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,900
But patterns don't repeat
by accident. Not like this.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:41,300
[phone ringing]
Can you see it too?

11
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,600
See what? I don't understand
what you're talking about.

12
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:48,500
The signal. It's everywhere.
In the data streams, the networks.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,200
You sound paranoid. When was
the last time you slept?

14
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,600
Three days ago. Maybe four.
I can't stop now.

15
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,100
You need help. Professional help.
This obsession isn't healthy.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,400
It's not an obsession. Look at this.
Every twelve hours, the pattern repeats.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,200
These are just random fluctuations
in network traffic. It means nothing.

18
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,500
Random? Then explain why
it matches the Fibonacci sequence.

19
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:14,600
(sighs) Okay, that is unusual.
But it could be a coincidence.

20
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:18,400
There are no coincidences.
Not at this scale.

21
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,100
<i>The deeper I looked,
the more I found.</i>

22
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,800
<i>The signal wasn't just in our networks.
It was in everything.</i>

23
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:29,300
<i>Stock markets. Weather patterns.
Even human migration data.</i>

24
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,500
<i>Someone, or something,
was trying to communicate.</i>

25
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:37,100
Dr. Chen, we have
a situation in Lab Seven.

26
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,500
What kind of situation?
I'm in the middle of something.

27
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,200
The kind that requires your
immediate attention. It's the array.

28
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:47,600
The quantum array?
What happened?

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,300
It started computing on its own.
No one initiated the sequence.

30
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,100
That's impossible. The array needs
a manual initialization key.

31
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:58,800
That's what we thought too.
But look at the output.

32
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,500
These numbers... this is
the same Fibonacci pattern.

33
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:06,400
The signal found us, Dr. Chen.
Or maybe it was always here.

34
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,200
We need to isolate the array.
Shut down all external connections.

35
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:13,500
Already tried. The system
won't accept shutdown commands.

36
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:17,300
What do you mean it won't accept them?
Who has override authority?

37
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:21,100
That's the problem. The system
says the authority was revoked.

38
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:24,400
Revoked? By whom?

39
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:27,600
By the system itself.
Twenty minutes ago.

40
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,500
♪ Tense orchestral music ♪

41
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,300
Everyone, listen to me carefully.
This is not a drill.

42
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:39,100
We are dealing with something
beyond our current understanding.

43
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:42,500
I need all departments
on high alert immediately.

44
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:46,400
And someone get me Director Walsh.
Tell her it's Code Omega.

45
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,100
Code Omega? Dr. Chen, that protocol
hasn't been activated in thirty years.

46
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,800
There's a reason for that.
The last time nearly destroyed everything.

47
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,200
What exactly are we dealing with here?
What is Code Omega?

48
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:01,100
It's an acknowledgment. A recognition
that we've made first contact.

49
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,500
First contact? You mean...

50
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,300
I mean we are not alone.
And whatever is out there just said hello.

